  "answer": "Low uridine salvage sensitizes cytarabine-residual AML to teriflunomide-induced differentiation\n\nHypothesis and premises. In oxidative, cytarabine-residual AML retaining differentiation blockade, insufficient adaptive uridine salvage relative to pyrimidine demand permits clinically attainable unbound teriflunomide to inhibit DHODH, deplete pyrimidines, and induce durable malignant-cell maturation with reduced leukemia-propagating capacity. Normal-progenitor sparing is predicted to depend on demonstrably greater salvage adequacy, not assumed normality. These drug-specific and selectivity links are speculative. DHODH-driven differentiation is supported by [S4]; oxidative residual-cell phenotypes without consistent stem-cell enrichment are supported by [S2]. Analyze residual cells separately from the ROS-low, BCL-2-associated stem-cell population described in [S1]. Venetoclax–azacitidine supplies an established comparator, not the proposed discovery [S3].\n\nDrug evidence and exposure gate. Unverified prior knowledge: teriflunomide is approved for relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis, inhibits DHODH, and uridine transport/uridine-cytidine kinase (UCK) enables salvage bypass. The packet cannot verify these drug-specific claims. Before efficacy experiments, verify approval, pharmacology, and licensed-dose human unbound peak, trough, AUC, and duration using authoritative drug-specific sources. Reproduce that numerical exposure envelope in protein-containing stromal coculture and xenografts, directly measuring free drug, including marrow exposure. Measure dilution-controlled marrow/plasma uridine and test the observed physiological range, including its upper end. Supraclinical efficacy cannot establish feasibility.\n\nAdaptive biomarker. Acute tracer flux is not maximal salvage capacity. In a separate reference-DHODH-inhibitor challenge, quantify absolute isotope-derived salvage flux, its adaptive increase and persistence across physiological uridine concentrations, and contemporaneous demand from macromolecular incorporation and irreversible loss. Report their time-resolved ratio R(t) as an operational balance, not a capacity estimate. Classify low reserve only when R<1 and sustained pool depletion persist despite adaptation, within windows preceding apoptosis or maturation. Treat the cutoff as provisional, analyze continuously, and validate independently. Record baseline and on-treatment cycling and demand so arrest-induced demand reduction is not mistaken for restored salvage reserve.\n\nCausality and novelty. In paired pretreatment/residual AML, inducibly increase or decrease experimentally verified limiting transport/UCK activity after cytarabine selection. Require reciprocal sensitivity shifts at matched free exposure and DHODH engagement, with repeated flux/demand measurements and drug-free controls. Test whether adaptive R improves held-out prediction beyond UCK expression, uridine uptake, proliferation, and respiration. Residual status is a context, not an assumed independent determinant: compare matched pretreatment cells and test for a residual-state interaction. Existing teriflunomide/leflunomide–AML and salvage-resistance literature must be checked before claiming novelty. Novelty remains unresolved; the candidate contribution is demand-adjusted, adaptive prediction in residual AML, not DHODH differentiation or salvage bypass themselves.\n\nSelectivity. Measure R trajectories, cycling, intracellular exposure, and DHODH engagement in matched normal CD34-positive progenitors. Compare cycle-matched subsets and perturb normal-cell salvage reciprocally. Attribute sparing to salvage only if these perturbations shift sensitivity after accounting for demand and exposure, without drug-free injury explaining the effect. Lower cycling or weaker engagement remains an alternative explanation. Assess normal colonies and hematopoietic engraftment.\n\nDiscriminating tests. Include vehicle, a characterized DHODH inhibitor, genetic DHODH suppression, and venetoclax–azacitidine. Establish engagement and pyrimidine depletion before apoptosis or differentiation. Begin uridine rescue before depletion; verify isotope-confirmed pool preservation with unchanged exposure/engagement. Delayed rescue after mapped differentiation commitment tests irreversibility and need not reverse maturation; supraphysiological rescue establishes mechanism, not marrow protection. Index-sort immature malignant cells, follow individual lineages using imaging and heritable barcodes, and confirm leukemia genotype in mature descendants. Measure blinded morphology, myeloid phenotype, washout-persistent maturation, apoptosis, viable output, and colonies separately. Randomized xenografts should undergo limiting-dilution secondary transplantation after verified drug washout using equal viable malignant-cell inputs; report total killing separately.\n\nAlternatives, falsification, and limitations. Off-target toxicity, arrest, selection of pre-existing mature cells, or microenvironmental salvage could mimic benefit. Prespecify donor-level effect thresholds. With sustained engagement/depletion at permitted exposure in low-reserve residual AML, absent lineage-proven maturation or unchanged viable-cell-normalized propagating frequency would reject the predicted differentiation-linked benefit. Differentiation despite prophylactic pool preservation contradicts the deprivation mechanism; failure of bidirectional salvage perturbations to shift sensitivity rejects salvage adequacy as the determinant. Failure to engage DHODH within the human envelope rejects translational feasibility, not DHODH biology. Marrow pharmacokinetics, adaptive demand, disease heterogeneity, incomplete normal-cell modeling, and unresolved prior-art overlap limit extrapolation. The packet establishes neither this drug’s therapeutic window nor clinical efficacy.",
